{{/* SR OS MD-CLI commands for this device's local half of an ELINE, in
MD-CLI's native block-paste form - the same shape "info"/"info inheritance"
prints and accepts back as input, sent one line at a time over a scripted,
non-interactive SSH session exactly the way a human pasting it would. See
driver_nokia_sros_eline.go for the surrounding candidate/commit session.

Cleanup first: one "delete" is enough since SR OS nests every SAP and
spoke-sdp binding inside its owning epipe. Shared by ApplyELINE (root
template includes this define) and RemoveELINE (renders "cleanup" alone).
Assumed benign no-op when name doesn't exist yet - not confirmed against
a real device. SDP tunnels are deliberately not deleted: every ELINE
toward the same neighbor shares them.

Both apply blocks lean on apply-groups ("eline-defaults" on the epipe,
"sdp-default" on the sdp) for everything that doesn't vary per service -
customer, service-mtu, control-word, vc-type, delivery-type, ldp, and every
nested object's own admin-state are all inherited from those groups rather
than set here (confirmed against a real device's "info inheritance" output -
see driver_nokia_sros_eline_test.go). Setting any of them explicitly here
would just needlessly duplicate what the group already guarantees.

.Name's epipe is deliberately owned end-to-end by this template: nothing
here is shared with another service, so a stale line left over from a
previous apply is always fully handled by that upfront delete - unlike
.SDPID's tunnel below, which every ELINE service toward the same neighbor
shares. This template only ever creates/merges it, never deletes it. */}}
{{define "cleanup"}}
delete /configure service epipe "{{.Name}}"
{{end}}
{{template "cleanup" .}}
/configure service epipe "{{.Name}}" {
    apply-groups ["eline-defaults"]
    description "{{.Description}}"
    service-id {{.ServiceNumericID}}
    sap {{.LocalIface}}:{{.LocalVLAN}}.* {
        description "{{.Description}}"
    }
{{if .Remote}}
    spoke-sdp {{.SDPID}}:{{.Remote.PseudowireID}} {
        description "PEER={{.Remote.DeviceName}} interface={{.Remote.RemoteIface}}.{{.Remote.RemoteVLAN}}"
    }
{{else}}
    sap {{.PeerLocalIface}}:{{.PeerLocalVLAN}}.* {
        description "{{.Description}}"
    }
{{end}}
}
{{if .Remote}}
/configure service sdp {{.SDPID}} {
    apply-groups ["sdp-default"]
    description "PEER={{.Remote.DeviceName}}"
    far-end {
        ip-address {{.Remote.NeighborIP}}
    }
}
{{end}}
